<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sorry, I Can't Help You.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of texts by answering both written and oral questions.<br>Reading: Identify key details in a story.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show understanding of polite request texts by writing simple responses (e.g., "Can I have a pencil?" "Yes, here.").<br>Can identify details in a story to answer questions (e.g., "Can she help her friend?" "No, she cannot.").<br>Can identify and blend individual phonemes to create one-syllable or two-syllable familiar words (e.g., /d-ɛ-s-k/ in the word "desk").</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of vocabulary words from Lesson 1 (e.g., <i>pencil, paper, desk, friend, bag, hand</i>). The teacher says each word clearly. Students repeat the words chorally. Then, the teacher shows a picture of a child needing help (e.g., looking for a pencil). The teacher asks, "What does she need?" and students point to the "pencil" flashcard or say "pencil". This activates prior knowledge and sets the context for needs and requests.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "Can I have a pencil, please?" The teacher points to each word while reading it aloud slowly and clearly. The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher then writes "I can't help you." and models its reading and the sound /k/ in "can't." The teacher claps out syllables for "pencil" and "friend" and asks students to listen for the individual sounds, blending them. (e.g., /p-ɛ-n-s-ə-l/ -> pencil; /f-r-ɛ-n-d/ -> friend)</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Can I have a pencil, please?', am I asking politely or rudely?" (Politely!)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I can't help you,' does that mean 'Yes, I can help' or 'No, I cannot help'?" (No, I cannot help.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short illustrated dialogue between two friends, Mia and Leo, focusing on a polite request and a polite refusal. The dialogue is displayed on a large chart paper or projected on the board. The text uses familiar vocabulary (<i>pencil, paper, friend, help, please, thank you, can, can't</i>) and grammatical structures (<i>Can I have...? please?, I can't help you.</i>).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br><b>Mia:</b> "Hello, Leo! Can I have a pencil, please?"<br><b>Leo:</b> "Hello, Mia! I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser. So, I can't lend it to you. I'm sorry."<br><b>Mia:</b> "Oh, okay. Thank you anyway, Leo!"<br><b>Leo:</b> "My pleasure. Can I help you find another pencil?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading Comprehension – The learner understands short, simple written texts on familiar topics.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud once. Students listen and follow along with their finger. Then the teacher asks, "Who are the friends?" (Mia and Leo). "What does Mia need?" (A pencil).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading (True/False):</b> The teacher rereads the dialogue sentence by sentence, pausing to ask "True or False?" questions.
              <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia wants an eraser. True or False?" (False, she wants a pencil).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can help Mia with a pencil. True or False?" (False, he can't because he doesn't have an eraser to go with it).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia says 'Thank you.' True or False?" (True).</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading polite requests ("Can I have...please?"), understanding polite refusals ("I can't..."), identifying key vocabulary (<i>pencil, paper, help, friend, ask, thank you</i>), and blending phonemes in familiar words (e.g., /d-ɛ-s-k/ in "desk", /k-aɪ-n-d/ in "kind").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Reading Practice (Matching):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with sentences from the dialogue and corresponding pictures. Students read each sentence and draw a line to match it to the correct picture (e.g., "Mia needs a pencil" -> picture of Mia with a thought bubble of a pencil; "Leo can't help her" -> picture of Leo shaking his head).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Reading Practice (Cloze Activity):</b> The teacher provides a slightly modified version of the dialogue with some key words missing (e.g., <i>pencil, please, can't, thank you, friend</i>). Students read the dialogue and fill in the blanks using a word bank provided at the top of the worksheet. The teacher guides by reading the complete sentences aloud after students finish. Focus on reading accuracy for blending sounds in words like "desk" if it appears in the text.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Partner Reading & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two small cards with simple questions about the dialogue (e.g., "What does Mia say?") and a card with the dialogue again. Student A reads Question 1 to Student B. Student B reads the dialogue to find the answer. Student B then tells or points to the answer in the text. Then they switch roles for Question 2. This activity encourages careful reading and cross-referencing within the text. The teacher circulates, observing reading and providing phonemic awareness correction or modeling if needed, e.g., "Listen to the /k/ sound in 'can't'."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Reading & Response. Students will independently read a new short story (3-4 sentences) about a different friend needing an item and receiving a polite refusal. Then, they will read and answer simple comprehension questions (multiple choice or short answer) based on the story.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Can You Read and Answer?"</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes a worksheet with a new short story, "Tom and the Paper," (e.g., "Tom is at his desk. He needs paper. He asks his friend, Sarah: 'Can I have some paper, please?' Sarah says: 'Sorry, Tom. I don't have paper in my bag. I can't help you.' Tom says: 'Oh, thank you anyway, Sarah!'").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the story silently and then aloud in a whisper voice to themselves.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the story, there are 2-3 comprehension questions (e.g., "Who needs paper?" A) Sarah B) Tom C) Desk. "Can Sarah give paper to Tom?" A) Yes, she can. B) No, she can't.). Students circle the correct answer or write a short response. This activity requires students to apply their reading comprehension skills to a new, but similar, text, demonstrating their understanding of polite requests and refusals.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Can You Read and Answer?" and review of student worksheets. The teacher will specifically look for evidence of students' ability to read for details and answer questions based on the text.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their completed "Can You Read and Answer?" worksheet from Stage 4. The teacher collects and quickly reviews their answers to the comprehension questions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying key details from the story (e.g., who needs what, whether help can be given).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written responses (even if one-word answers or circled options).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated understanding of polite request/refusal context through correct answers.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Before leaving, students complete an exit ticket by drawing a happy or sad face next to these statements on a slip of paper:
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read a story about friends asking for things."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the answers to questions in a story."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the story was easy for you today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words did you learn to read today?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., The illustrated dialogue helped students visualize the text; the matching activity reinforced comprehension of key sentences.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking)? (e.g., Ensure all students are confident recognizing and pronouncing the core vocabulary before moving to speaking activities. Perhaps incorporate more choral reading to build confidence before individual speaking.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided students with exposure to polite requests and refusals in written form. In Lesson 3, students will actively use these phrases and vocabulary in spoken interactions, practicing making and responding to polite requests with their peers.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>